WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.470
Thanks to KiwiCo for supporting this episode
of Journey to the Microcosmos!

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:13.530
Click the link in the description to learn
more and for a special offer.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:22.630
“Bursaria truncatella is a giant ciliate.”

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:27.690
That is a quote, the first sentence, in fact,
from a scientific paper called “The Gravitaxis

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:35.260
of Bursaria truncatella: Electrophysiological
and behavioral analyses of a large ciliate cell.”

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:40.140
If you’re not quite sure what that title
means, don’t worry, we’ll get to it.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:47.890
I will occasionally have an argument with
my four year old about the size of microbes.

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:53.109
We’ll look under the microscope and at a
mere 40x magnification, whatever we’re looking

00:00:53.109 --> 00:00:59.559
at might fill the whole view, I will shout
“It’s huge” and he will retort “it’s

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:04.830
tiny!” and then we will laugh because, of
course, we are both right.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:10.920
But with their ability to grow to around 1
mm in length, Bursaria truncatella and its

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:15.380
fellow Bursaria relatives are giant ciliates.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:16.380
Right?

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.830
They are certainly giant /for/ a ciliate.

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:24.330
But I would argue beyond that...i think, though
my son will fight me on it, that all things

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:25.670
are relative.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:32.200
And if a blue whale is a giant of our world,
then Bursaria are giants of theirs.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.670
So giant that you can see them with the unaided
human eye.

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:41.140
A single cell that emerges from the microcosmos
into our world.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:47.230
Now there are times when being a giant seems
to work out quite well for Bursaria, especially

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:53.670
because its large body size is matched by
its large mouth, shaped like a horn or, as

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:56.200
others have noted, like a baseball glove.

00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:05.350
And that large mouth comes in handy when it
comes to ingesting other ciliates

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:10.429
If the word “bursaria” sounds familiar
to you, it might be thanks to Paramecium bursaria,

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:14.700
another ciliate that we’ve come across in
our journey through the microcosmos.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:19.129
And While Bursaria and Paramecium bursaria
are technically related in the sense that

00:02:19.129 --> 00:02:21.980
they’re both ciliates, they are not closely
related.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:37.510
And a Bursaria will absolutely go ahead and
eat a paramecium when it feels like it.

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:42.189
The origin of their shared name comes from
the Latin “bursa,” which translates to

00:02:42.189 --> 00:02:43.519
purse.

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:48.150
Handbag designs these days come in all sorts
of shapes and sizes, but I suppose with a

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:53.650
generous image of a purse in mind, you might
stare at these organisms and consider them

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:56.430
bags full of biology.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:59.760
This naming, by the way, isn’t even restricted
to microbes.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.500
There’s a genus of plants named Bursaria
because of the shape their fruit comes in.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:09.430
So, apparently when it comes to nature, scientists
are always just seeing purses everywhere.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:10.430
Anyway.

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:16.970
Returning to our current Bursaria of interest:
even giants have to find ways to protect themselves.

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:22.890
And when you put a ton of resources into creating
that massive body, you want to protect that

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:26.280
body from the environmental whims of the microcosmos.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:31.349
Maybe it’s getting dry, or cold, or there
isn’t much food.

00:03:31.349 --> 00:03:36.540
Many microbes, in situations like this, will
make a cyst...a little house it can use to

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.290
ride out whatever storm has come their way.

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:44.049
We’ve seen a bunch of different microbial
cysts so far in our journey, all with their

00:03:44.049 --> 00:03:46.529
own distinctive shapes and sizes.

00:03:46.529 --> 00:03:51.670
And if we were to compare these cysts and
award them superlatives, the Bursaria’s

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:56.029
would have to be “most resembles the top
of a cartoon diamond.”

00:03:56.029 --> 00:04:01.459
The sphere in the middle is called the endocyst,
and it holds the resting body of the Bursaria.

00:04:01.459 --> 00:04:07.079
The endocyst is encased in a multi-faceted
exterior, connected by little bridges that

00:04:07.079 --> 00:04:09.559
hold the layers of the cyst together..

00:04:09.559 --> 00:04:12.930
These cysts are reported to be around 100
microns long.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:18.829
And remember, the active Bursaria is around
1 mm, so 1,000 microns long.

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:25.060
Just imagine the work that goes into collapsing
that giant ciliate into a cyst that is a tenth

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:31.040
of its size, and also the work that goes into
remaking the giant when it emerges.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:36.530
The excystment process, it’s said to be
incredible to watch, taking anywhere from

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:42.669
2 to 6 hours as the Bursaria pushes against
an opening of the cyst until it can finally

00:04:42.669 --> 00:04:47.320
pop through and emerge, unscathed but also
unrecognizable.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:52.990
It takes about an hour for the cilia and organelles
to arrange themselves in their appropriate

00:04:52.990 --> 00:05:00.580
places, allowing the Bursaria to resume its
active life.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:04.699
Being a giant comes with other challenges
too, like gravity.

00:05:04.699 --> 00:05:08.729
Now, you and I are always dealing with gravity,
whether we like it or not.

00:05:08.729 --> 00:05:10.440
It’s, it’s just...there.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:14.159
Keeping us rooted to the ground, making us
fall down when we trip.

00:05:14.159 --> 00:05:19.449
But for free-swimming ciliates like Bursaria,
they’re not worried about tripping and falling.

00:05:19.449 --> 00:05:25.350
They’re worried about sinking, the density
of their cytoplasm pulling their bodies down

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:31.330
through the less dense freshwater around them
and causing them to sediment.

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:32.910
That is a verb in this case.

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:37.940
It means getting dragged down to the bottom
and becoming, basically, sediment.

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:43.110
To make sure they don’t sink, these organisms
have to be able to figure out what direction

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:48.639
gravity is pulling them in, and then counteract
that force by swimming.

00:05:48.639 --> 00:05:53.250
And scientists have been observing unicellular
organisms doing this for centuries.

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:57.520
There are a few different ways microbes move
through their environment.

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:02.370
They may move in response to sensing chemicals,
which we call chemotaxis.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:06.510
They might move in response to light, which
we call phototaxis.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:10.039
And then there’s moving in response to sensing
gravity.

00:06:10.039 --> 00:06:15.039
Maybe you have a guess at what that might
be called...gravitaxis.

00:06:15.039 --> 00:06:19.979
We used to call this geotaxis, a term that
some people still use even though it is way

00:06:19.979 --> 00:06:23.629
more confusing because they are stubborn and
they do not like change.

00:06:23.629 --> 00:06:26.170
And yes, we are willing to fight this fight.

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:27.870
Geotaxis is right out.

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:32.590
We’re sticking with gravitaxis.

00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:38.689
When a microbe is traveling down but not swimming,
it is sedimenting.

00:06:38.689 --> 00:06:42.810
Scientists measure the sedimentation rate
of microbes by immobilizing them and watching

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:44.550
them fall through water.

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:49.330
And using that method, they’ve found that
the sedimentation rate of Paramecium tetraurelia

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:56.710
is about 67.4 micrometers per second, and
the sedimentation rate for Paramecium caudatum

00:06:56.710 --> 00:06:59.580
was about 123 micrometers per second..

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:03.139
So that gives you a baseline for some ciliates.

00:07:03.139 --> 00:07:06.460
Bursaria truncatella’s sedimentation rate?

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:09.740
923 micrometers per second.

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:12.340
I repeat: 923.

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:16.139
That’s almost a millimeter every second.

00:07:16.139 --> 00:07:21.310
That is because of its large volume, which
is several hundred times that of Paramecium

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:22.790
tetraurelia.

00:07:22.790 --> 00:07:28.659
So Bursaria really have to swim hard to make
sure they don’t just sink to the bottom

00:07:28.659 --> 00:07:30.419
of a pond.

00:07:30.419 --> 00:07:32.639
And that swimming is no joke.

00:07:32.639 --> 00:07:38.380
Bursaria may have cilia, but there’s a physical
limit to how many cilia they can fit on their,

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:40.000
yes massive, bodies.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:44.749
So they really have to take advantage of what
they have to generate the forces necessary

00:07:44.749 --> 00:07:46.509
to counteract sedimentation.

00:07:46.509 --> 00:07:50.719
Of course, it’s not enough just to swim.

00:07:50.719 --> 00:07:54.860
They have to make sure they’re swimming
in the right direction, otherwise they may

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:57.560
just be hastening their own descent.

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:03.069
And that is why it is so vital for them to
be able to sense the direction of gravity.

00:08:03.069 --> 00:08:04.069
How do they do it?

00:08:04.069 --> 00:08:10.539
Well, we just don’t completely know how
Bursaria and other microbes do gravitaxis.

00:08:10.539 --> 00:08:15.009
Scientists have studied the organisms under
both microgravity and hypergravity to see

00:08:15.009 --> 00:08:16.009
how they respond.

00:08:16.009 --> 00:08:20.229
What is most important to realize though is
that there aren’t very many things microbes

00:08:20.229 --> 00:08:27.319
can sense, and adding gravity to that list
has enabled these microscopic giants to thrive

00:08:27.319 --> 00:08:29.009
in their worlds.

00:08:29.009 --> 00:08:31.379
Would we like to know how they do it?

00:08:31.379 --> 00:08:32.379
Absolutely we would.

00:08:32.379 --> 00:08:36.800
And we know how Paramecium do it better than
we understand bursaria’s gravitaxis.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:43.440
But to get to the bottom of it, we as a species
need to keep searching and learning and discovering.

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:45.060
That’s what we do.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:54.050
The way microbes have chemotaxis and gravitaxis,
humans maybe have curiotaxis.

00:08:54.050 --> 00:09:00.910
We can sense what we do and do not know...and
we can allow ourselves to be drawn away from

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:08.340
or toward those things.

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:13.120
Thanks for being one of the people who is
drawn toward the things that we do not know,

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:19.900
and thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

